---
// src/pages/guides/agave-care.astro
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const businessName = import.meta.env['PUBLIC_BUSINESS_NAME'] || 'Palm & Agave Nursery';
---

<Layout
  title="Agave Selection and Care — Plant Guides | Palm & Agave Nursery"
  description="How to choose, plant, and care for agaves in Florida landscapes. Sun exposure, drainage, sizing, cold tolerance, and what to expect as agaves establish in Hillsborough County."
>
  <Header cartCount={0} />

  <div class="guide-page">
    <article class="guide-article container">
      <header class="guide-header">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="breadcrumb-list">
            <li><a href="/" class="breadcrumb-link">Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li><a href="/guides" class="breadcrumb-link">Guides</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="breadcrumb-current" aria-current="page">Agave Care</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Agave Care</p>
        <h1 class="guide-title">Agave selection and care for Florida landscapes</h1>
        <p class="guide-meta">A practical guide for homeowners, landscape contractors, and designers working with agaves in Hillsborough County and the Tampa Bay region.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="guide-content">
        <p class="guide-intro">
          Agaves bring architectural structure, clean lines, and long-lived presence to Florida landscapes. They are not maintenance-free, but they are low-maintenance when placed correctly. The most important decisions happen before planting — sun, drainage, space, and species choice.
        </p>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Choosing the right agave for your space</h2>
          <p>
            Agaves vary widely in size, growth rate, leaf shape, and cold tolerance. A compact agave in a 10-gallon container can remain small for years, while a specimen agave in a 25-gallon container may already be a mature focal point. The right choice depends on the space, the design intent, and how much room the plant will have as it matures.
          </p>
          <p>
            For smaller residential spaces, compact and architectural agaves work well as feature plants, entry accents, or container specimens. For larger landscapes and commercial properties, specimen agaves and landscape-ready agaves can anchor a design or provide structure across a planting plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            The catalog filters by the attributes that matter most: container size, rosette diameter, sun exposure, cold tolerance, and variegation. Use those filters before selecting a plant rather than relying on common name alone.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Sun exposure</h2>
          <p>
            Most agaves perform best in full sun or bright partial shade. Full sun produces tighter rosettes, stronger leaf color, and more reliable growth. Partial shade can work for some species, especially in hotter exposures or where afternoon sun is intense, but too much shade can lead to loose, weaker growth and reduced form.
          </p>
          <p>
            In Hillsborough County, full sun is generally sufficient for most agave species we stock. If a plant is described as tolerating partial shade, that usually means it can handle filtered light or morning sun with afternoon shade — not deep shade.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Drainage and soil</h2>
          <p>
            Drainage is the single most important soil factor for agaves. They do not tolerate standing water or consistently wet soil. In Florida, where rainfall can be heavy and soils vary, drainage often matters more than soil fertility.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plant agaves in well-drained soil. If your site has heavy clay, flat areas that hold water, or a high water table, improve drainage or choose a raised bed or container. For container growing, use a well-draining mix and a pot with drainage holes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Agaves are not heavy feeders. Avoid rich, water-retentive soils and frequent fertilization. A lean, well-drained setup is closer to what most agaves prefer.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Watering</h2>
          <p>
            Agaves are drought-tolerant once established, but they still need regular watering during the first growing season. The goal is to help the plant establish roots without keeping it wet.
          </p>
          <p>
            A practical approach:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Water newly planted agaves regularly for the first several weeks, then gradually reduce frequency.</li>
            <li>Allow the soil to dry between waterings. Agaves prefer a cycle of moisture followed by dryness.</li>
            <li>In Florida's rainy season, natural rainfall may be enough for established plants in well-drained sites. Watch for standing water.</li>
            <li>In containers, water more frequently than in the ground, but still let the soil dry between waterings.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Overwatering is a more common problem than underwatering for agaves in residential landscapes. If in doubt, lean toward less water rather than more.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Cold tolerance</h2>
          <p>
            Cold tolerance is one of the most important selection factors for Florida landscapes. It refers to the approximate lowest temperature a plant can handle, often given as a Fahrenheit range. This is a general guide, not a guarantee — actual survival depends on duration, humidity, wind, plant health, and how established the plant is.
          </p>
          <p>
            Hillsborough County generally experiences mild winters, but cold snaps do happen. If you are planting in an exposed location, near the coast, or in an area that tends to be cooler, choose a species with additional cold tolerance. If you are planting in a warmer microclimate, you may have more flexibility.
          </p>
          <p>
            The catalog lists cold tolerance where known. When in doubt, choose a plant with better cold tolerance than you think you need rather than one at the edge of its range.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Sizing and measurements</h2>
          <p>
            Agave listings include the container size, current rosette diameter, and current plant height. These are practical measures of what you are buying now, not the plant's final size.
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Container size</strong> — the pot the plant is growing in. This is the most reliable indicator of the plant's current scale and the size you can actually transport and plant.</li>
            <li><strong>Rosette diameter</strong> — the width of the agave's rosette. This is the measurement that matters most for spacing and design.</li>
            <li><strong>Plant height</strong> — the height of the plant from the base to the top of the leaves. This can change with growth, leaf orientation, and watering.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Mature height and spread are given where known, but agaves vary. Use mature size as a planning reference, not a promise. A compact agave may stay small, while a specimen agave may eventually become a substantial plant.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Handling and safety</h2>
          <p>
            Many agaves have sharp terminal spines or marginal teeth. Some are relatively smooth, while others are genuinely hazardous to handle without care. Always check the plant's spine characteristics before placing it near walkways, play areas, pools, or anywhere people will brush against it.
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Wear gloves and eye protection when handling large or spiny agaves.</li>
            <li>Keep spiny agaves away from paths, driveways, and high-traffic areas.</li>
            <li>Use the plant's spine characteristics and common name as a guide, but inspect the actual plant when possible.</li>
            <li>Children, pets, and barefoot traffic are good reasons to choose a smooth-leafed agave or place a spiny one carefully.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Planting</h2>
          <p>
            Plant agaves at the same depth they were growing in the container. Do not bury the base of the plant deeper than it was already growing. In fact, planting slightly high can help in wetter sites because it improves drainage around the crown.
          </p>
          <p>
            Steps for planting in the ground:
          </p>
          <ol class="guide-list guide-list--ordered">
            <li>Choose a location with appropriate sun exposure and good drainage.</li>
            <li>Dig a hole slightly wider than the root ball, not substantially deeper.</li>
            <li>Remove the plant from its container carefully. Agave roots are generally not aggressive, but handle the plant to avoid damaging the crown or leaves.</li>
            <li>Place the plant so the base is at or slightly above the surrounding soil level.</li>
            <li>Backfill with native soil or a well-draining mix, firm gently, and water in.</li>
            <li>Apply a light layer of mulch around — but not against — the base if desired, and keep mulch away from the crown.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            For container planting, use a pot with drainage holes and a well-draining mix. Do not use a container that is much larger than the plant needs — agaves generally prefer to be somewhat root-bound rather than sitting in excess soil and moisture.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Long-term care</h2>
          <p>
            Once established, agaves need relatively little care. The main tasks are occasional inspection, removing dead or damaged leaves, watching for drainage issues, and protecting the plant during cold events if needed.
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Inspect periodically for pests, damage, and drainage problems.</li>
            <li>Remove dead or withered lower leaves as needed, taking care with spiny species.</li>
            <li>Avoid overwatering and over-fertilizing.</li>
            <li>In containers, repot only when the plant has outgrown its container, and use a well-draining mix.</li>
            <li>Some agaves produce offsets over time. Offsets can be removed and rooted if desired, but not all agaves offset readily.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Common mistakes</h2>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Too much water.</strong> The most common problem in Florida landscapes. Agaves in wet soil decline faster than dry ones.</li>
            <li><strong>Poor drainage.</strong> Planting in flat, wet, or clay-heavy areas without improving drainage leads to root problems.</li>
            <li><strong>Too much shade.</strong> Agaves in deep shade grow poorly and lose form.</li>
            <li><strong>Too close to traffic.</strong> Spiny agaves placed near walkways create hazards.</li>
            <li><strong>Over-potting.</strong> Putting a small agave in a very large container leads to excess moisture and root issues.</li>
            <li><strong>Confusing container size with mature size.</strong> A 10-gallon agave is not a 10-gallon mature plant. Use mature size for planning, container size for what you are buying now.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section guide-section--cta">
          <p class="guide-cta-text">
            Ready to choose an agave? Browse our agave catalog, filter by the attributes that matter for your space, and contact us if you need help matching a plant to a specific landscape.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="/shop/agaves" class="btn btn-primary">Shop Agaves</a>
            <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-outline">Ask a Question</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-page {
    padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-16);
  }

  .guide-article {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .breadcrumb-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .breadcrumb-link {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .breadcrumb-link:hover,
  .breadcrumb-link:focus-visible { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .breadcrumb-current { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-medium); }

  .guide-eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: var(--font-semibold);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--color-sage);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .guide-title {
    font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 4vw, var(--text-4xl));
    color: var(--color-green-deep);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .guide-meta {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    max-width: 580px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .guide-content {
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
  }

  .guide-intro {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
  }

  .guide-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
  }

  .guide-section-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--color-green-deep);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .guide-section p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .guide-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .guide-list li {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    position: relative;
  }

  .guide-list li::before {
    content: '—';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--color-sage);
  }

  .guide-list--ordered {
    counter-reset: guide-ordered;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .guide-list--ordered li {
    counter-increment: guide-ordered;
  }

  .guide-list--ordered li::before {
    content: counter(guide-ordered) '.';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--color-green-deep);
    font-weight: var(--font-semibold);
  }

  .guide-section--cta {
    padding: var(--space-8);
    background-color: rgba(41, 79, 59, 0.04);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .guide-cta-text {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .guide-cta-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
</style>
